<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mihirakula</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 540 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mihirakula was a ruler of the Alchon <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a> who controlled parts of north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in the early sixth century. He is remembered in Indian sources as a cruel persecutor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>. Hunnic invasions under rulers such as Mihirakula contributed to the decline of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gupta-empire">Gupta Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mihirakula.jpg" alt="Mihirakula" width="320" height="342" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CNG coins. Contoured by me पाटलिपुत्र · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mihirakula_portrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Mihirakula, also written Mihiragula or Mahiragula, was the son and successor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toramana">Toramana</a> and the second and last king of the Alchon Huns in the north-west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a>. His name is probably Iranian: Janos Harmatta rendered it as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithra">Mithra</a>'s Begotten, while the Sanskritized form can be read as mihira, sun, and kula, clan. The 6th-century Alexandrian traveller <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cosmas-indicopleustes">Cosmas Indicopleustes</a> described a lord of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> called Gollas, thought to be Mihirakula, who went to war with no fewer than two thousand elephants and a great force of cavalry.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Around 520 the Chinese Buddhist monk Song Yun met Mihirakula, by then master of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a>, and wrote that he did not believe in any religion, that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmins</a> of his kingdom disliked him and that his people were exhausted by his wars. The Gwalior inscription of his 15th regnal year shows that his rule reached Gwalior. His coins show <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oesho">Oesho</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a>, and some <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> coins carry a bull with the words &quot;The bull should be victorious&quot;, which may point to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaivism">Shaivism</a>.</p><p>The 7th-century pilgrim <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xuanzang">Xuanzang</a> tells, as hearsay, that Mihirakula ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sagala">Sagala</a>, now Sialkot, and turned against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> when the monks sent him a servant instead of a learned teacher. In this account he was defeated and captured by the Gupta king Narasimhagupta Baladitya of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magadha">Magadha</a>, spared at the plea of Baladitya's mother, and then seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a> and Gandhara, demolishing 1600 stupas and monasteries. His campaigns ended with his defeat by the Aulikara king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yasodharman">Yashodharman</a> in the Battle of Sondani, partly the subject of the Mandsaur Pillar inscriptions.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xuanzang">Xuanzang</a>, Mihirakula died a sudden death. The 12th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajatarangini">Rajatarangini</a> calls him a man of violent acts resembling kala, death, and Buddhist and later Jaina texts remember him as a tyrant. Modern scholars are more cautious. Jason Neelis and Shōshin Kuwayama find no evidence that he destroyed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> in Gandhara, where monasteries went on growing for centuries, and the monastery at Harwan near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srinagar">Srinagar</a> was built in this period. Upinder Singh asks whether his reputation reflects real persecution, or the fact that his rival Baladitya was an ardent patron of the Buddhist sangha.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mihirakula” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>By around 500 CE, the Hunas under Toramana and subsequently <b>Mihirakula</b> had occupied large portions of northwestern and central India, and Mihirakula is remembered in Buddhist sources for his brutality towards monasteries.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gupta-empire">Gupta Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the Guptas, who favoured Brahmins but tolerated other faiths, great monasteries such as Nalanda became centres of learning, though the Huna king <b>Mihirakula</b> destroyed many others in the early 6th century.</blockquote><a href="/societies/classical-india">Social Hierarchy of Classical India</a></li><li><blockquote>His son <b>Mihirakula</b> ruled from Sakala and was remembered by Buddhist writers such as Xuanzang as a persecutor who destroyed monasteries.</blockquote><a href="/state/alchon-huns">Alchon Huns</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 528 Yashodharman set up victory pillars at Sondani near Mandsaur, claiming to have defeated the Hun ruler <b>Mihirakula</b> and to rule lands never held by the Guptas.</blockquote><a href="/state/aulikara-dynasty">Aulikara dynasty</a></li><li><blockquote>His sequence is interrupted by Turushka kings, apparently the Kushans, and by the Hun ruler <b>Mihirakula</b>, so some names may preserve real memories, but history and legend are thoroughly mixed.</blockquote><a href="/state/gonanda-dynasty">Gonanda dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
